Z||000000||DaRkLoRD||06-19-2001||12:33 AM||||for a project I need a way to power 2 electrodes with a variable frequency.. I've been working on a design (haven't built it yet) using a 555 timer, with a pot to adjust the resistance to get a variable output pulse, which will switch a mosfet, therefore pulsing the battery power to the electrodes. I think it will be a fairly simple project, but I've never done anything with ICs before.. anyone see any problems with this idea? doesn't have to be perfect, just a cheap way of getting a variable frequency.. range is whatever I can get with under $20 parts.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> (very primitive prototype)<P>can anyone help, or build one and sell it to me cheap?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000001||FieroJoe||06-19-2001||12:52 AM||||It sounds to me that the 555 setup you are using should work.<P>Though all my 555 experience has been with monostable mode, I think I remember reading that in astable mode the determining factor of your output pulse is the resistances in the circuit.<P><BR>This site has a good tutorial, in addition to a bunch of technical jargon.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <A HREF="http://www.uoguelph.ca/~antoon/gadgets/555.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.uoguelph.ca/~antoon/gadgets/555.htm</A> <P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
